{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Therefore she called his name Dan.</b> Every tribe regarding which the expression ״עַל כֵּן״ (\"therefore\") was said was numerous in population, except for the tribe of Levi, because the Ark consumed them. And know this: the expression ״עַל כֵּן״ was said only regarding Levi, Judah, and Dan, for ״בְּרָב עָם הַדְרַת מֶלֶךְ״ (Proverbs 14:28) — and Judah is ״גּוּר אַרְיֵה״ (Genesis 49:9); from him is the splendor of kingship; and ״דָּן יָדִין עַמּוֹ״ (Genesis 49:16) — he will take vengeance for his people ״כְּאַחַד שִׁבְטֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל,״ that is, like David the king, who was unique among the tribes; and one who takes vengeance for his people also needs population. And for this reason it is said (Deuteronomy 33:22) ״דָּן גּוּר אַרְיֵה״ — they likened him to David, who was also likened to a lion's whelp. And the tribe of Levi — from it came the crown of Torah and the crown of priesthood, for Moses received the Torah and Aaron the priesthood; all the more so should the tribe of Levi have been numerous, for from it came the ministers of our Elohim, and 'in the multitude of people is their splendor' — but the Ark consumed them. There are those who say that because they were close to the Ark, they separated from their wives, and therefore did not multiply so greatly. And they raise a difficulty with this: were they not few even before the burden of the Ark was given to them? Therefore they explained that this ark was the ark of Jacob. But there is no need for this, for in any event it was the Ark of the Holy that was the cause: because Levi was destined to bear the Ark of the Holy, therefore Jacob said that Levi would not bear his ark, since in the future he would bear the Ark of the Holy. And therefore the Egyptians treated the tribe of Levi with honor, for they saw that Jacob treated him with honor, and therefore the Egyptians did not enslave them and they were not included in ״פֶּן יִרְבֶּה״ (Exodus 1:10), and therefore they were also not included in ״כֵּן יִרְבֶּה״ (Exodus 1:12). And the reason for this is: everyone who was included in ״פֶּן יִרְבֶּה״ — whom the Egyptians sought to diminish out of malice and trouble — the Holy One, blessed be He, needed to show them that all their wisdom availed them nothing against the will of HaShem, blessed be He, and He breached and multiplied them beyond the way of the world. But the tribe of Levi, who were not in the Egyptian oppression and whom they did not seek to diminish — the Holy One, blessed be He, had no need to multiply them against nature; therefore they were not so numerous in population. And it appears that for this reason as well the tribe of Levi were poor and had no inheritance in the land, for all who suffered the punishment of ״כִּי גֵר יִהְיֶה זַרְעֲךָ וַעֲבָדוּם וְעִנּוּ אֹתָם״ (Genesis 15:13) were included in ״וְאַחֲרֵי כֵן יֵצְאוּ בִּרְכֻשׁ גָּדוֹל״ (Genesis 15:14) — and this is easy to understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:20.522761814Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}